# Line endings are pinned, because this repository builds one file by
# concatenating others byte-for-byte.
#
# build.py reads the numbered sources out of the WORKING TREE and writes
# worker_entry_combined.js from those exact bytes. That makes the build a
# function of whatever line endings the checkout happens to have -- so on a
# checkout that materialised CRLF (Windows, core.autocrlf=true) the build
# produced a CRLF Worker, and on Linux an LF one, from identical sources.
#
# That is how the two ended up disagreeing: the sources are committed LF and
# worker_entry_combined.js was committed CRLF. Nothing caught it, because
# verify.sh and CI both compare with `git diff --ignore-cr-at-eol`. What did
# notice was check_sync.py, which compared raw bytes and therefore failed on a
# clean checkout -- and told you to run build.py, which would have committed
# 3.3MB of line-ending churn and flipped the file back again for the next
# person on the other platform.
#
# eol=lf rather than `text=auto` alone: normalising in the repository is not
# enough here, because the build reads the working tree. The checkout itself
# has to be LF on every platform for the build to be reproducible.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Generated -- `python3 build.py` from header.js + the numbered sources.
# Never edit it by hand; CI rebuilds it and fails on drift. Marked generated so
# GitHub collapses its 3.3MB by default in reviews, where it is noise next to
# the sources it was built from.
worker_entry_combined.js linguist-generated=true

